{"maxId":70,"entries":{"chapter-1-study-biology-of-aging-and-longevity|Chapter 1 Study: Biology of Aging and Longevity":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Aging predisposes to multiple diseases and loss of homeostasis; delaying a single illness is not equivalent to slowing the aging process (p3-4).":["u3"],"copy|Dietary and single-gene manipulations can postpone multiple age-associated disorders together in animals (p4-5).":["u4"],"copy|Rodents fed 30–40% less typically live up to 40% longer; benefits depend on diet composition, timing and model (p5).":["u5"],"copy|Dietary restriction improves human metabolic risk measures, but whether restriction without malnutrition extends human longevity is unknown (p6).":["u6"],"copy|Insulin/IGF signaling and FoxO connect nutrient availability with stress resistance; effects vary by tissue and organism (p7-8).":["u7"],"copy|TOR regulates growth, translation and protein quality control; autophagy removes damaged material (p8-9).":["u8"],"copy|Sirtuins link NAD availability to protein modification and metabolism; mammalian family members have distinct effects (p9-10).":["u9"],"copy|Stress resistance, genome maintenance and proteostasis overlap; antioxidant treatment does not reliably extend lifespan in model organisms (p10-12).":["u10"],"copy|Telomere loss can limit cell replication; senescence intersects with tumor suppression, tissue dysfunction and cancer. Cell-culture senescence is not the whole aging process (p13-14).":["u11"],"copy|Mouse longevity effects may depend on sex, genetic background and laboratory conditions (p14-16).":["u12"],"copy|Rapamycin, canagliflozin, acarbose and estradiol-related interventions extend mouse survival in the studies discussed; these are experimental longevity findings, not human dosing recommendations (p17).":["u13"],"copy|Genetic factors account for about 15–20% of human lifespan variation; disease susceptibility and longevity are difficult to separate (p17-19).":["u14"],"copy|APOE associations replicate more consistently than many candidate longevity loci; a healthy-aging phenotype is more informative than age at death alone (p19).":["u15"],"copy|Werner and Hutchinson–Gilford syndromes mimic selected aging features but also differ substantially from normal aging (p20).":["u16"],"copy|The book finds no evidence that marketed supplements such as melatonin, DHEA or resveratrol retard human aging; the research goal is longer healthy life, not merely prolonged disability (p21-22).":["u17"],"exam-questions|Exam questions":["u18"],"copy|CHAPTER 1s / HAZZARD":["u19"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 1; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u20"],"copy|Aging is modifiable in model organisms":["u21"],"copy|Interacting biological pathways":["u22"],"copy|Translation and its limits":["u23"],"copy|Past-paper questions linked to this chapter appear in the Exam questions tab.":["u24"],"copy|CHAPTER 1 / HAZZARD":["u19"]},"sequenceOffsets":[[71,-46],[117,0]]}
